{"id":"covenant","name":"Covenant","aliases":["Covenant"],"sources":["nave","torrey","easton"],"topics":["nave:covenant","torrey:covenant-the"],"groups":["Of Men With Men","National See ALLIANCES","Instances Of","Of God With Men","Of Man With God"],"definition":{"source":"easton","term":"Covenant","text":"A contract or agreement between two parties. In the Old Testament the Hebrew word berith is always thus translated. Berith is derived from a root which means “to cut,” and hence a covenant is a “cutting,” with reference to the cutting or dividing of animals into two parts, and the contracting parties passing between them, in making a covenant (Gen. 15; Jer. 34:18, 19). The corresponding word in the New Testament Greek is diatheke, which is, however, rendered “testament” generally in the Authorized Version. It ought to be rendered, just as the word berith of the Old Testament, “covenant.” This word is used (1) of a covenant or compact between man and man (Gen. 21:32), or between tribes or nations (1 Sam. 11:1; Josh. 9:6, 15). In entering into a convenant, Jehovah was solemnly called on to witness the transaction (Gen. 31:50), and hence it was called a “covenant of the Lord” (1 Sam. …"},"lemmas":[{"strong":"H1285","lemma":"בְּרִית","xlit":"bᵉrîyth","gloss":"a compact","kjv":"confederacy, feder, covenant, league","count":112,"share":0.3},{"strong":"H3772","lemma":"כָּרַת","xlit":"kârath","gloss":"to cut","kjv":"be chewed, be con- ate, covenant, cut","count":44,"share":0.12},{"strong":"H5769","lemma":"עוֹלָם","xlit":"ʻôwlâm","gloss":"concealed","kjv":"alway, ancient, any more, continuance","count":34,"share":0.09},{"strong":"H7650","lemma":"שָׁבַע","xlit":"shâbaʻ","gloss":"to seven oneself","kjv":"adjure, charge, feed to the full ), take an oath","count":24,"share":0.06},{"strong":"H2233","lemma":"זֶרַע","xlit":"zeraʻ","gloss":"seed","kjv":"carnally, child, fruitful, seed","count":24,"share":0.06},{"strong":"G1242","lemma":"διαθήκη","xlit":"diathḗkē","gloss":"a disposi